    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Most wood decks need to be resealed every 1 to 3 years: a clear water repellent usually needs a fresh coat every year, a penetrating oil sealer or toner typically lasts about 2 to 3 years, and a semi-transparent sealer-stain can stretch toward 3 years on a sheltered deck. The deck floor almost always wears out before the railings, so the walking surface is what sets your schedule. Those ranges are not a calendar you have to obey. A shaded deck in a mild climate can go longer than the label suggests, while a south-facing deck in hot sun, snow country or heavy foot traffic can need attention sooner. The reliable way to know is to test the wood, not count the months. This guide covers the typical interval for each type of sealer, what shortens or stretches it, the two-minute water test, and how to plan maintenance coats so you never have to strip the deck back to bare wood.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="by-type" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How Often Does Each Type of Deck Sealer Need Reapplying?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The single biggest factor in how often you seal a deck is what you sealed it with last time. Deck sealers are not one product. They range from thin, clear water repellents that soak into the top layer of the wood to tinted penetrating oils and semi-transparent sealer-stains that carry pigment. The more solids and pigment a product leaves in the wood, the longer it tends to hold up, and the less often you have to repeat the job.
          </p>

          <p>
            Clear water repellents are the shortest-lived. They are designed to make water bead and run off, and they do that well when fresh, but the active ingredients sit near the surface where sun and foot traffic wear them away. On a horizontal deck surface in full sun, most clear repellents need recoating every year, and some deck owners in hot climates find water soaking in again before the end of the first summer.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Sealer type</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical resealing interval (deck floor)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What wears out first</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Clear water repellent</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About every 12 months</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Water beading on high-traffic paths and sunny boards</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Clear penetrating oil sealer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 1 to 2 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Graying, because clear has little UV protection</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Toner or lightly tinted oil sealer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 2 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Color fading on the sunniest boards</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Semi-transparent sealer-stain</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 2 to 3 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Color and water repellency in traffic lanes</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Solid or opaque deck stain</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 3 to 5 years (see our stain guide)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Peeling or wear on steps and walkways</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Penetrating oil sealers and toners sit in the middle. Because they soak deeper and often contain some pigment, they usually last around two years on a deck floor, and three on a sheltered one. Semi-transparent sealer-stains add enough pigment to block much more UV light, which is why they often reach the two-to-three-year mark. Treat every number in the table as a typical range, and check the wear life printed on your product's label, which is the manufacturer's own estimate for normal conditions.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are not sure what is on your deck now, look at it closely after a rain. A deck where water beads but the wood has gone silver was probably sealed with a clear repellent. A deck that still holds some color but lets water soak in has a pigmented finish that has lost its repellency. Knowing which you have tells you both how soon to reseal and whether you can keep using the same product. Our <a href="/deck-stain-vs-sealer/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">deck stain vs sealer</a> guide explains the difference in more detail.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="why-it-wears" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Why Does Deck Sealer Wear Out So Fast?
          </h2>

          <p>
            A deck is one of the harshest places you can put a wood finish. Unlike siding or furniture, the floor boards face the sky directly, collect standing water and snow, and take shoe soles, dog claws, grills and furniture legs every day. Every one of those wears away the thin layer of sealer that sits in the top fibers of the wood. Nothing about a deck sealer failing early is a sign you did it wrong. It is the nature of the location.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ultraviolet light is the main enemy. Sunlight breaks down lignin, the natural glue that holds wood fibers together, at the surface of the board. That is what turns unprotected wood gray. A clear sealer can keep water out but cannot stop UV, so the wood underneath keeps graying and the loose, weathered fibers on top shed the sealer along with them. Pigment is the only practical UV blocker in a deck finish, which is why tinted products tend to last longer.
          </p>

          <p>
            Water does the rest. A board that soaks up rain swells, then shrinks as it dries in the sun, over and over. That movement opens small checks and cracks, especially on flat-sawn boards and on the end grain, and pushes the sealer out of the pores. Freeze-thaw cycles in cold climates make it worse, because water that gets into a crack expands when it freezes and widens the opening for the next storm.
          </p>

          <p>
            Foot traffic is the final factor, and it is uneven. The path from the back door to the stairs, the area around the grill and the top steps wear through long before the corners under a bench. That is why most decks do not fail all at once. They fail in lanes, and a good sealing schedule is built around catching those high-wear lanes before they go fully bare, rather than waiting for the whole deck to look bad.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="water-test" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. How Can You Tell When It Is Time to Reseal?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The water test is the most useful two minutes in deck maintenance. On a dry day, sprinkle a small amount of water, about a cup, on several boards in different spots: one in a traffic lane, one in full sun, one in a sheltered corner. Watch what happens. If the water beads up and sits on top, the sealer is still doing its job. If it soaks in and darkens the wood within a few minutes, that area needs resealing.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Beads up: wait. Soaks in: reseal.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Test the sunniest board and the busiest traffic lane, not the sheltered corner. Those spots fail first, and they tell you when the whole deck is due.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Test several spots because the results will almost always differ. It is common for a deck to bead perfectly under the eaves and soak water like a sponge near the stairs. When the high-wear areas start absorbing, that is your signal. You do not need to wait until the entire deck fails, and waiting usually means more prep work, because the wood in the worn areas has had time to gray, check and grow mildew.
          </p>

          <p>
            Visual signs back up the water test. Graying wood, a dull or chalky look, small splinters lifting on the surface, black spots of mildew and color that has faded unevenly between boards all point to a sealer that has stopped protecting. On a pigmented finish, look for the color thinning out in traffic lanes while it still looks rich along the edges. That contrast means it is time for at least a maintenance coat.
          </p>

          <p>
            A good habit is to run the test twice a year, once in spring before the deck season starts and once in early fall before the weather turns. The spring test tells you whether to reseal before summer sun. The fall test catches a finish that wore out over the summer, giving you a window to recoat while the temperatures are still in the range most sealer labels call for, usually about 50 to 90 degrees Fahrenheit.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="horizontal-vertical" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Do Railings Need Sealing as Often as the Deck Floor?
          </h2>

          <p>
            No. Vertical surfaces like railings, balusters, posts and skirt boards usually hold a finish noticeably longer than the floor. Rain runs off them instead of pooling, the sun hits them at an angle rather than straight on, and nobody walks on them. Many finish manufacturers note that vertical surfaces can last roughly twice as long as horizontal ones, and that matches what most deck owners see in practice.
          </p>

          <p>
            That difference matters for your schedule. If you seal the whole deck every year because the floor needs it, you will eventually build up too much product on the railings. Penetrating sealers are designed to soak in, and once the wood is saturated, extra product sits on top, stays tacky and can peel or look blotchy. The fix is simple: run the water test on the railings separately and only recoat them when they actually start absorbing.
          </p>

          <p>
            A practical approach is to seal the floor, stairs and the tops of the railing caps on the short schedule, and the vertical balusters and posts on a longer one. Railing caps behave like floor boards because they face the sky and hold water, so they belong with the floor. The balusters underneath can often go every other cycle, which saves product, time and the tedious brushing that railings require.
          </p>

          <p>
            Stairs are the exception in the other direction. Treads take more foot traffic per square foot than any other part of the deck, collect grit from shoes and often stay wet in the shade. Expect stair treads and the landing at the bottom to need resealing first. If your budget or time is tight, spot-treating the treads and the main traffic lane in a year when the rest of the deck still beads is a reasonable compromise.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="climate" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How Do Sun, Climate and Shade Change the Schedule?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sun exposure moves the needle more than anything except the product itself. A south- or west-facing deck in full afternoon sun can lose its sealer in half the time of a deck shaded by trees or the house. In the high-UV parts of the country, from the desert Southwest to the high plains and mountain states, plan on the short end of every range and consider a pigmented product rather than a clear one.
          </p>

          <p>
            Wet climates wear decks differently. In the Pacific Northwest, the Southeast and anywhere with long, humid summers, the problem is less UV and more constant moisture, mildew and algae. A sealer in those regions may still bead water on schedule but lose the battle against black mildew spots and green growth in shaded areas. Cleaning matters as much as resealing there, and products with mildewcides can help keep the surface clean between coats.
          </p>

          <p>
            Snow country adds freeze-thaw and shoveling. Snow sitting on a deck for weeks keeps the boards wet, and metal shovel blades scrape the finish off the top of the boards. Decks in cold climates often look fine in the fall and worn out by April. If that sounds like your deck, test in spring rather than fall, and use a plastic shovel or a broom on the deck surface during winter.
          </p>

          <p>
            Shade is a double-edged factor. Shaded decks keep their color longer because they see less UV, but they dry more slowly after rain and are more prone to mildew. A deck under heavy tree cover may only need sealing every three years for UV, yet need a good cleaning every spring to remove mildew, pollen and leaf stains. Follow a routine cleaning with the water test, and only reseal when the test says so.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="maintenance-coats" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. What Is a Maintenance Coat, and Do You Have to Strip First?
          </h2>

          <p>
            A maintenance coat is a single light coat of the same product applied over a sealer that is worn but not fully gone. It is the cheapest and easiest way to keep a deck protected, and the main reason not to wait too long between coats. If you reseal while the old finish is thin but intact, you usually only need to clean the deck, let it dry, and apply one coat. No stripping and no sanding.
          </p>

          <p>
            The prep for a maintenance coat is still important. Sweep off debris, clean the surface with a deck cleaner to remove dirt, mildew and loose gray fibers, rinse thoroughly and let the deck dry for at least a day or two. Our <a href="/how-to-clean-a-deck/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">guide to cleaning a deck</a> walks through the steps. Sealer applied over dirt or mildew bonds to the dirt instead of the wood and fails fast, which shortens your schedule for no reason.
          </p>

          <p>
            Stick with the same type of product when you can. Most oil-based penetrating sealers can go over themselves without trouble, and many water-based products can go over themselves too. Switching from one type to a very different one, such as putting a water-based product over a fresh oil, or any penetrating sealer over a film-forming stain that has not been removed, can cause poor absorption and peeling. When in doubt, check the label of the new product for what it can be applied over.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not overdo it. The most common way to make a deck look worse is to pile coat after coat of penetrating sealer on wood that is already saturated. If a test spot shows the old sealer still beading, skip those areas, or wipe off any excess that does not soak in within the time the label gives. A thin coat that soaks in fully will last longer than a thick coat that sits on top and gets tacky, and it is ready for feet and furniture much sooner. Our guide to <a href="/how-long-does-deck-sealer-take-to-dry/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">how long deck sealer takes to dry</a> covers the wait times for each type.
          </p>

          <p>
            Stripping is only needed when the old finish is failing unevenly, peeling, or you are changing to an incompatible product. That is a much bigger job that usually means a stripper, a pressure washer or scrub brush, a brightener to restore the wood's pH, and sometimes sanding. Keeping up with maintenance coats on schedule is how you avoid that weekend entirely, and it is the strongest argument for resealing a little early rather than a little late.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sealer-vs-stain" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Is Sealing a Deck on a Different Schedule Than Staining It?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Yes, and the difference is mostly about pigment. A clear sealer or toner protects against water but does little against sun, so it needs reapplying more often. A stain adds pigment that blocks UV and hides graying, so it usually lasts longer before it needs attention. The more opaque the finish, the longer it lasts on the surface, but also the more work it takes to remove when it eventually fails.
          </p>

          <p>
            That trade-off is why many deck owners move from clear to tinted over time. A clear sealer on a new deck shows off the fresh wood, but by year three or four the wood has usually grayed under the sealer and an annual recoat starts to feel like a chore. Switching to a semi-transparent sealer-stain at that point adds color, slows the graying and often stretches the schedule to two or three years. Our guide on <a href="/how-often-to-stain-a-deck/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">how often to stain a deck</a> covers the pigmented side in detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are choosing a product with the schedule in mind, think about how much maintenance you are realistically willing to do. A clear water repellent is cheap and easy to apply but commits you to a yearly coat. A good penetrating oil sealer or semi-transparent sealer-stain costs more per gallon, but you buy it and apply it less often. Our roundup of the <a href="/best-deck-sealant/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best deck sealants</a> compares how long each lasts in practice.
          </p>

          <p>
            Composite and PVC decks are a separate case. Most do not need sealing at all, and some manufacturers warn against it because a sealer can leave a slippery or blotchy film. Hardwoods like ipe need a specific penetrating oil if you want to keep their color, often more than once a year in full sun, or no finish at all if you are happy to let them gray. Everything in this guide applies to softwood decks: pressure-treated pine, cedar and redwood.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing on the calendar, not the condition</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Resealing every spring whether the deck needs it or not builds up excess product on boards that are still saturated. That extra sealer sits on the surface, stays sticky and can peel. Run the water test first and only recoat the areas that soak up water, especially if you used a longer-lasting penetrating oil.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Waiting until the whole deck is gray</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                By the time the entire deck looks worn, the traffic lanes have usually been bare for a season, with graying, checking and mildew in the wood. That means stripping, brightening and sometimes sanding instead of a simple clean and recoat. Reseal when the busiest areas start absorbing water, not when the corners finally give up.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating railings like the floor</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Railings and balusters often last twice as long as the deck surface. Recoating them every time you recoat the floor saturates the wood and leaves a blotchy, tacky buildup. Test vertical surfaces separately. Seal the railing caps with the floor, and do the balusters and posts only when they start absorbing water.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the cleaning step</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Sealer applied over dirt, pollen, mildew or loose gray fibers bonds to that debris instead of the wood. It can look fine for a few weeks, then wear away early, which makes your next coat due sooner. Clean the deck with a proper deck cleaner, rinse well and let it dry fully before every coat.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Applying in the wrong weather</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Sealing in hot midday sun, on a damp deck or right before rain shortens the life of the coat from the start. Most labels call for temperatures around 50 to 90 degrees, a dry deck and a rain-free window afterward. A poorly cured coat fails sooner and throws off your whole schedule.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you seal a deck too often?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Penetrating sealers only work when they can soak into the wood. If you apply a new coat while the old one is still repelling water, the new product has nowhere to go and dries on the surface as a sticky or shiny film that can peel or attract dirt. Only recoat areas that fail the water test, and wipe off excess.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should you seal a new deck right away?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually not. New pressure-treated lumber is often too wet to absorb sealer, and many new boards have a smooth mill surface that resists penetration. Most guidance is to wait until the wood passes a water test, which can take weeks to a few months depending on the lumber and the weather. Kiln-dried lumber can typically be sealed sooner.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What time of year is best to reseal a deck?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Late spring and early fall are usually best because temperatures sit in the range most labels call for and there is less risk of extreme heat. Avoid sealing when nights drop near freezing or when the forecast shows rain within a day. Many people reseal in spring so the deck is protected before the summer sun.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a deck need sealing every year?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if you use a clear water repellent or your deck takes heavy sun and traffic. Penetrating oil sealers and semi-transparent sealer-stains typically last two to three years on a deck floor. Run the water test each spring and fall, and reseal when the high-traffic and sunniest boards start soaking up water instead of beading it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do you need to seal a cedar deck?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Cedar has natural rot resistance, but it still grays, checks and absorbs water without a finish. If you want to keep its color and limit cracking, seal it on roughly the same schedule as other softwood decks, typically every one to three years depending on the product. If you are happy with gray, cedar can go without, though it will weather faster.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long should a deck dry before resealing?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                After cleaning or rain, most manufacturers want the deck dry for at least 24 to 48 hours before sealing. Damp wood will not absorb sealer properly, and moisture trapped under the coat can cause poor adhesion or a cloudy look. Check the label for specifics, and test a spot by sprinkling water on it before you start.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it bad to never seal a deck?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                An unsealed wood deck will not fall apart quickly, especially if it is pressure-treated, but it will gray, splinter, crack and grow mildew faster. Boards may cup and warp from repeated wetting and drying. Sealing every few years is inexpensive compared with replacing boards, and it keeps the surface comfortable for bare feet.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

