        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Most decks need resealing every one to three years. Clear water repellents sit at the short end and usually need a fresh coat every year, penetrating oils and toners typically last about two years, and semi-transparent sealer-stains can reach three. Full sun, heavy traffic and harsh winters push you toward the short end, while shade and mild weather buy you time.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            The better habit is to test rather than guess. Sprinkle water on the sunniest boards and the busiest path every spring and fall, and reseal when it soaks in instead of beading. Catching the deck at that point means a simple clean and one thin coat, not a weekend of stripping, and that is what keeps the job cheap and the wood in good shape for decades.
          </p>
        </div>

